a vaccinated population in Brazil</article-title><source><italic>Transactions of the Royal Socity of Tropical Medicine and                        Hygiene</italic></source><year>1998</year><volume>92</volume><fpage>227</fpage><lpage>230</lpage></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="ref017"><label>17</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><name><surname>Enquselassie</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><etal/><article-title>Seroepidemiology of measles in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia:                        implications for control through vaccination</article-title><source><italic>Epidemiology and Infection</italic></source><year>2003</year><volume>130</volume><fpage>507</fpage><lpage>519</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12825737</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="ref018"><label>18</label><mixed-citation publication-type="other"><collab>WHO</collab><comment>. Vaccine Preventable Disease unit of WHO/African Region, Vaccine                        Preventable Diseases Bulletin WHO/AFRO. AFRO EPI-Newsletter, October, </comment><year>2001</year></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="ref019"><label>19</label><mixed-citation publication-type="book"><collab>WHO</collab><italic>Report on the 1st Consultation of the Technical Advisory Group on                        Measles and Rubella Control in the African Region</italic><publisher-loc>Harare, Zimbabwe</publisher-loc><comment>: World Health Organisation Regional Office for Africa, </comment><year>2005</year></mixed-citation></ref><ref id="ref020"><label>20</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><collab>CDC</collab><article-title>Effects of measles-control activities &#x02013; African region,                        1999&#x02013;2005</article-title><source><italic>Morbidity and Mortality Weekly Report</italic></source><year>2006</year><volume>55</volume><fpage>1017</fpage><lpage>1021</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16988641</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref></ref-list></back></article>